  "verdict": "(b) — the NULLs were written by the database when the parent rows were deleted, NOT by the app writing NULL. Deleting the demo clients fired ON DELETE SET NULL on client_id and CASCADE-deleted the bank_accounts rows, which then emptied bank_account_id. The subject_client_id anchor column survived on all 18 rows, which is the designed behaviour from migration 101 and means the rows are still traceable. This is the safety net working, not a data-loss bug.",
